# The Oblivion Ring ruling's involuntary loop, which ends the game in a draw (CR 104.4b, 732.4). Nobody but Bot1 has a
# nonland permanent. Turn 5: Ring A's enters ability has no legal target ("another target nonland permanent": the
# only nonland permanent is A itself) and is removed from the stack (CR 603.3d); A exiles nothing. Turn 7: Ring B's
# enters ability has exactly one legal target, A, so it is chosen without asking: A is exiled with B, and A's leaves
# ability returns nothing (A exiled nothing). Turn 9: Ring C's enters ability has one legal target, B (A is in exile):
# B is exiled with C, so B's leaves ability triggers alone and returns A, a new object (CR 400.7). A's enters
# ability has one legal target, C (B is in exile): C is exiled with A, C's leaves ability returns B, B's enters ability
# targets A, its only choice, and so on: each Ring's leaves ability and the next Ring's enters ability trigger one at a
# time, never together (each enters only as the previous leaves ability resolves), so there is no order to choose; every
# target is the lone legal one; and at every priority neither player can do anything but pass or tap a land for mana
# (Bot1's hand is only Plains, Bot2's only Islands, nothing is castable with a trigger on the stack). The position -
# two Rings on the battlefield, one holding the third in exile, the next trigger on the stack - comes round again with
# no player having made a choice: a loop of mandatory actions with no way to stop it. Once it has come round three times
# with every player only passing, the game is a draw (docs/rules-exceptions.md, RX-4).

[Bot1 Deck]
Oblivion Ring
Oblivion Ring
Oblivion Ring
Plains
Plains
Plains
Plains
Plains x12

[Bot2 Deck]
Island x20

